 ## 2026-07-28
 
+### 0612 — Board replanned to the macOS prototype path; 202 autonomous tasks behind one ceremony (TASK-260728-3a2dnr)
+- DECISION: Owner-approved strategy encoded in `.spec/decisions.md` as ADR-023 (M0 SSH viability scope, Option A, with the four deferred semantics named and owned by TASK-260728-3cveay) and ADR-024 (macOS-only physical target, iOS deferred, Linux CI nonblocking, Relux Works signing/notary available). ADR-013 re-scoped from a blocking prototype gate to a release-path gate; ADR-014 moved Open → Accepted with libssh2 as the primary engine.
+- ANOMALY: `task-board validate` reported one dependency cycle, but an independent DFS over all 397 elements found **eight**. The validator stops at the first cycle it reaches, so a clean-looking single-cycle report can hide a cluster. Always re-traverse the live graph after a cycle fix rather than trusting the next validate run alone.
+- FINDING: The root cause of the M1 deadlock was a contract/integration inversion, not a milestone-ordering mistake. `TASK-260715-1pn983` (binding memory ledger) was blocked by `30ugfm` (M1 routing integration) and `z37ay7` (M2 UDP enforcement) — both are work the ledger is supposed to *govern*. Removing those two edges and adding `z37ay7 blockedBy 1pn983` broke two cycles and kept the reviewer-added `3miqh4 ← 1pn983` accountability gate intact; `productionAuthorization` stays `false`.
+- FINDING: The macOS path was silently gated on iOS through five non-obvious edges — `1idq8c`, `1lmmri`, `1uxx3i`, and `sbrrp7` all waited on `33oofa` (iOS targets), and `3lab1f` waited on `2hiabd` (iOS provider adapter). Those four alone blocked every macOS UI task, the macOS release archive, and all macOS physical provider validation. Deferring a platform is not just skipping its tasks; the cross-platform "shared" tasks have to be re-rooted too.
+- DECISION: Ceremony C1 deliberately includes the Gate P0 system-VPN approval click. The disposable probe builds in minutes from profiles created minutes earlier, so it stays one sitting. Hold H2 (real app, different bundle identifier) cannot be folded in and remains the next human boundary after 202 autonomous tasks.
+- FINDING: `set_status(..., status=to-dev)` fails on an unassigned element. Container status mismatches were therefore repaired by downgrading 16 stories to `backlog` rather than promoting tasks — which is also more honest, since promoting whole stories would have marked deferred iOS/A0 tasks as ready.
+- VERIFICATION: `task-board validate` → valid, 0 issues (was 46). Independent cycle traversal → 0. No source tree change: `git status --porcelain -- Sources Tests Protocol relay scripts config Package.swift Makefile` is empty. Invariant scan confirms host-key-before-auth, Keychain-only secrets, fail-closed DNS, bounded memory, public packetFlow bridge, rootless exec/stdio relay, and signed/notarized release all survive the edit. Evidence: `.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-3a2dnr/`.
+
 ### 0421 — Security-testing review now routes to the provider that previously filter-failed on it (TASK-260728-1glezz)
 - FINDING: The new serial policy makes review Codex-owned and marks security, networking, DNS, parsers, signing, and release tasks `review=required`. The superseded `docs/spawn-policy.md` section it replaced recorded that Codex `gpt-5.6-sol` trips OpenAI's cybersecurity content filter on legitimate defensive security-testing work (fuzzing, hostile-input corpora, DNS/route leak tests, redaction tests) and exits non-zero mid-run.
 - ANOMALY: Removing that section was required by the task AC, so the highest-risk review categories are now routed to the provider with the known failure mode. Whether the filter still reproduces on `gpt-5.6-sol` is unverified — no security-testing review has run under the new policy yet.

